WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:36.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. Back in May 2018, reporter Olivia Humphreys had her first baby. She remembers being in London, an allowed chaotic postnatal ward waiting to go home. And it was during this time that she was handed a small drawstring bag. Inside it, there was a little towel with a brand logo on it, a few tiny samples of baby shampoo and nappy cream, and a ton of coupons. For lots of products, I'd never. heard of, but apparently desperately needed now that I had a new baby.
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马尔斯。2018年5月，记者奥利维亚·汉弗莱斯生下了她的第一个孩子。她记得当时在伦敦，身处一个混乱的产后病房，等待着回家。就在那时，有人递给她一个小抽绳袋。袋子里有一条印着品牌标志的小毛巾、几份婴儿洗发水和尿布霜的小样，还有一大堆优惠券。很多产品我闻所未闻，但显然，既然有了新生儿，我就迫切需要它们。

2
00:00:36.000 --> 00:01:05.000
It's always nice to get free stuff, but the bag didn't make me feel better. It just added to the sense that I was woefully under-prepared. It was in those first sleepless nights after she returned home with her son, that Olivia started to really consider her bag of coupons. Her mind drifted to a better alternative. And she wondered if there was anywhere in the world where new parents were sent home with something more substantial. And that's when I found out what new parents in Finland receive.
能拿到免费的东西总是件好事，但这个袋子并没有让我感觉好受些。它反而加深了我那种极其缺乏准备的感觉。在带着儿子回家后的那些不眠之夜里，奥利维亚开始真正审视她那袋优惠券。她的思绪飘向了一个更好的选择。她想知道世界上是否有什么地方，会让新手父母带着更实在的东西回家。就在那时，我发现了芬兰的新手父母会收到什么。

3
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:33.000
A very exciting day for us. Our Finnish Baby Box has arrived. Our package is here. We get two because we have twins. In Finland, new parents also get a package of freebies. But instead of a few sample-sized products and some advertising flyers, they get something else. The Finnish maternity package, or the baby box. So let's see what is in here.
对我们来说是非常激动人心的一天。我们的芬兰婴儿盒到了。我们的包裹到了。因为我们生的是双胞胎，所以我们收到了两个。在芬兰，新手父母也会收到一个免费礼包。但他们得到的不是几份试用装产品和一些广告传单，而是别的东西。芬兰的产妇礼包，也就是婴儿盒。让我们来看看里面有什么。

4
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:46.000
T'da. Good Lord, there's so much stuff in here. This is a blanket. Wow. This is beautiful. Oh my God, it's got bears on it. So soft. Very soft.
当当。天哪，这里面的东西真多。这是一条毯子。哇。这太漂亮了。天哪，上面还有小熊。好软。非常柔软。

5
00:01:46.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Oh my gosh. Very, very cute. And look, a little toothbrush. They don't have any teeth. Why is this for? Later. For later. A Finnish Baby Box is a little bit of.
天哪。非常非常可爱。看，还有一把小牙刷。他们还没长牙呢。这是干什么用的？以后用。以后用。芬兰婴儿盒有点像……

6
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:38.000
is essentially a large cardboard box full of items for a newborn and its parents. Picture books, nipple cream, warm booties for the freezing finish winters. Basically a starter kit of everything you'll need for the first six months of a baby's life. Virtually every new parent in Finland is eligible to receive one. My first thought when I heard this was, of course, it's exactly what you'd expect of Finland, the happiest country on earth, with its widely revered education system and comprehensive welfare states. The Simpsons even has a whole episode making fun of just how freaking happy the Finns are.
本质上是一个装满了新生儿及其父母所需物品的大纸箱。绘本、乳头霜、适合芬兰严寒冬天的保暖靴。基本上就是你照顾宝宝前六个月所需一切的入门套件。芬兰几乎每一位新手父母都有资格领取一个。当我听到这个消息时，我的第一反应当然是：这正是你对芬兰的期待——世界上最幸福的国家，拥有广受推崇的教育体系和全面的福利国家制度。《辛普森一家》甚至有一整集都在调侃芬兰人到底有多幸福。

7
00:02:38.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Oh, geez, I can't afford this. No, it's all free. Free? Who are you people? Well, in Finland, we're not only experts on nuclear power. But for six years in a row, we've been the happiest people in the world. Makes sense. She lives so close to Santa Claus. But there's more to the baby box than a nice perk for the first. the happiest parents in the world. In its almost 90 years of existence, the Finnish baby box has been
噢，天哪，我可买不起这个。不，这全是免费的。免费？你们是什么人？嗯，在芬兰，我们不仅是核能方面的专家，而且连续六年被评为世界上最幸福的人。说得通。她住得离圣诞老人那么近。但婴儿盒不仅仅是给世界上最幸福的父母的一项福利。在近90年的历史中，芬兰婴儿盒一直……

8
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:41.000
a basic survival tool, a national symbol, and a right of passage. So how did a simple cardboard box become so filled with meaning? To find out, we're going to take a peek inside the cutest little social engineering tool in the world. So the story of the baby box begins in Finland in the late 1930s. Finland wasn't in such great shape. That's design historian Michelle Miller Fisher. She told me that when the first baby box came out, Finland was almost unrecognizable. The birth rate was declining.
是一个基本的生存工具、一个国家象征，也是一种成人礼。那么，一个简单的纸箱是如何被赋予如此多意义的呢？为了找出答案，我们将一窥这个世界上最可爱的社会工程工具。婴儿盒的故事始于20世纪30年代末的芬兰。当时的芬兰状况并不好。这是设计历史学家米歇尔·米勒·费舍尔说的。她告诉我，当第一个婴儿盒问世时，芬兰几乎让人认不出来。出生率在下降。

9
00:03:41.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Many babies that were born were in ill health and there was quite a high rate of infant mortality. In the early decades of the 20th century, most Finns were extremely poor. Finland had recently emerged from a brutal civil war and revolutions in neighbouring Russia meant it had also lost its biggest trading partner. Since most of the grain in Finland came from Russia,
许多新生儿健康状况不佳，婴儿死亡率相当高。在20世纪初的几十年里，大多数芬兰人极其贫穷。芬兰刚从一场残酷的内战中走出来，邻国俄罗斯的革命也意味着它失去了最大的贸易伙伴。由于芬兰的大部分谷物来自俄罗斯，

10
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:34.000
that loss meant that many people were left starving. Finns were living in crowded, unsanitary conditions, and dying from outbreaks of tuberculosis, smallpox, and measles. Child mortality in Finland was also higher than in the rest of Europe. Almost 20% of children didn't make it to their fifth birthday. And so the Finnish Social Welfare Committee decided, that maybe they could design something that would help in part to change this. And that policy intervention looked like, in the end,
这种损失意味着许多人陷入饥饿。芬兰人生活在拥挤、不卫生的环境中，死于肺结核、天花和麻疹的爆发。芬兰的儿童死亡率也高于欧洲其他地区。几乎20%的儿童活不到五岁。因此，芬兰社会福利委员会决定，也许他们可以设计一些东西，在一定程度上帮助改变这种状况。最终，这项政策干预看起来就像……

11
00:04:34.000 --> 00:05:00.000
a set of designed objects within a box. The idea of distributing critical items to new mothers wasn't an entirely original one. Since the 1920s, a Finnish charity had been lending out baskets of baby essentials to the poorest families in Finland. The baskets contained clean baby clothes, hand-sown by volunteers, from donated bits of fabric. At the time, it was pretty common for mothers
一套装在盒子里的设计物品。向新妈妈分发关键物品的想法并非完全原创。自20世纪20年代以来，一家芬兰慈善机构一直在向芬兰最贫困的家庭出借装有婴儿必需品的篮子。篮子里装着志愿者用捐赠的碎布缝制的干净婴儿衣服。当时，母亲们……

12
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:19.000
to swaddle their newborns in old rags, and this gave them an alternative. Once the baby grew out of the clothes, the basket was passed on to the next family who needed it. The government used the baskets as a blueprint for something more ambitious. They kept the clean clothes and added basic hygiene items, like soap and towels,
用旧破布包裹新生儿是很常见的，而这为她们提供了一种替代方案。一旦婴儿长大了穿不下这些衣服，篮子就会传给下一个需要的家庭。政府将这些篮子作为蓝图，用于更宏大的计划。他们保留了干净的衣服，并增加了基本的卫生用品，如肥皂和毛巾，

13
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:49.000
to help slow the spread of disease. And instead of loaning it out, They decided to make the box a gift that families could keep. In 1938, they started giving these new packages out to struggling families, not in a basket, but in a cardboard box, a box that doubled as a crib. It has been from the day one that you could put your baby to sleep in the box. That's a Nina Kwoka. She works for Keller, the Finnish Social Security Agency.
以帮助减缓疾病的传播。他们决定不再出借，而是将盒子作为一份家庭可以保留的礼物。1938年，他们开始向困难家庭发放这些新礼包，不是用篮子，而是用纸箱，一个可以兼作婴儿床的盒子。从第一天起，你就可以让宝宝睡在盒子里。这是安妮娜·克沃卡。她在凯拉（芬兰社会保险局）工作。

14
00:05:49.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Actually, in the early days, the families were poor, and their houses were small. There wasn't necessarily a safe and clean place to put the baby to sleep. So that's why it was quite important. When World War II broke out just one year later, Finland didn't have fabric to spare for the baby boxes. So the designers had to get creative.
实际上，在早期，家庭很贫穷，房子也很小。不一定有一个安全、干净的地方让宝宝睡觉。所以这就是它相当重要的原因。仅仅一年后，当第二次世界大战爆发时，芬兰没有多余的布料来制作婴儿盒。所以设计师们必须发挥创造力。

15
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:52.000
During the moment of the war years in the 1940s, because of restrictions on materials and because of thrift, some of the clothes were paper rather than, fabric. Clean paper clothes were still better than old rags, and a simple sheet of paper could make a big difference during childbirth. In some of the very early baby boxes, there was also the sheet of paper to give birth on, to be able to create an immediate sterile birthing environment in a very low cost, but very effective manner. When the war ended, Finnish mothers could thankfully put aside their paper baby clothes. Post-war boxes included fabric and yarn, so moms could simply
在20世纪40年代的战争岁月里，由于材料限制和节俭，一些衣服是纸做的，而不是布做的。干净的纸衣服仍然比旧破布好，一张简单的纸在分娩时也能发挥很大作用。在一些非常早期的婴儿盒里，还有一张用来分娩的纸，以便以非常低成本但非常有效的方式创造一个即时的无菌分娩环境。战争结束后，芬兰母亲们谢天谢地可以把纸质婴儿服收起来了。战后的盒子里包括了布料和纱线，这样妈妈们就可以……

16
00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:34.000
use all their free time to sew or knit the clothes themselves. Around this time, Finland also made a change that would become a key feature of the baby box. In 1949, they made the box a universal benefit. It was initially given out to people who needed it, and very quickly from the 1940s became given to anybody, everybody. It became a citizen's right in Finland. Most of us are probably familiar with the Nordic countries and their universal. welfare programs. Denmark, Sweden, Norway, Iceland and Finland all provide stronger social support than most other countries in the world. And in Finland, the baby box was one of the first building
利用所有的空闲时间自己缝制或编织衣服。大约在这个时候，芬兰还做了一项改变，后来成为婴儿盒的一个关键特征。1949年，他们将婴儿盒变成了一项全民福利。它最初是发给有需要的人，但从20世纪40年代开始，很快就变成了发给任何人，每个人。它成为了芬兰公民的一项权利。我们大多数人可能都熟悉北欧国家及其全民福利计划。丹麦、瑞典、挪威、冰岛和芬兰提供的社会支持都比世界上大多数其他国家要强。在芬兰，婴儿盒是建立该体系的第一块……

17
00:07:34.000 --> 00:08:22.000
blocks in creating that system. The Finnish welfare state as we know it began in the aftermath of World War II. Finland, like the rest of Europe, was still recovering from the devastation of war. People had come back from the brink of the end of the world, and across the continent, they asking themselves, what do we want? What do we value now that we have a chance to make a new world? In Finland, one of the strongest new convictions was that families, all families, should have support. Some of the earliest universal policies were focused on child rearing, free school meals, quarterly child payments, and new maternal health clinics opening all over the country. The baby box was actually one way to incentivize pregnant women to use those new health clinics.
基石之一。我们所知的芬兰福利国家始于二战之后。芬兰和欧洲其他国家一样，仍在从战争的破坏中恢复。人们从世界末日的边缘归来，在整个欧洲大陆，他们都在问自己：我们想要什么？既然我们有机会创造一个新世界，我们重视什么？在芬兰，最坚定的新信念之一是家庭，所有家庭，都应该得到支持。最早的一些全民政策集中在育儿、免费学校午餐、季度儿童津贴，以及在全国各地开设新的孕产妇健康诊所。婴儿盒实际上是激励孕妇使用这些新健康诊所的一种方式。

18
00:08:22.000 --> 00:09:00.000
In order to get the box, they would have to attend the maternity and welfare clinics and to get their health checked when they were expecting a baby. The lure of the baby box seems to have worked. By the early 1950s, more than 90% of mothers had registered themselves at the new clinics. Almost 80 years later, the deal is basically the same. If you visit a prenatal clinic in the first four months of pregnancy, you get the box. What's inside the box, though, changes every year. Should we start with the oldest box?
为了得到盒子，她们必须参加孕产妇和福利诊所，并在怀孕时进行健康检查。婴儿盒的诱惑似乎奏效了。到20世纪50年代初，超过90%的母亲在新的诊所注册。近80年后，这笔交易基本没变。如果你在怀孕的前四个月去产前诊所检查，你就能得到盒子。不过，盒子里的东西每年都在变。我们要从最旧的盒子开始看吗？

19
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:20.000
I visited the office of Keller, the government agency responsible for the box. They've got 40 years' worth of boxes stored away in their warehouse. So this is from 1994. So more than 30 years old. This is the snow seat. It's very 90s. Yeah, the patterns. Patterns and fabrics.
我参观了负责婴儿盒的政府机构凯拉的办公室。他们的仓库里储存了40年的盒子。这是1994年的。所以有30多年了。这是雪地服。非常有90年代风格。是的，图案。图案和面料。

20
00:09:20.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Lime green stripes and fluorescent yellow. Yeah, it's very 90s. Today, Anina and her colleague Vera are responsible for deciding what each year's baby box will include. And it's a lot of pressure. Every item they pick out, be it a koala sleeping bag or a dinosaur toothbrush, will be used by thousands of families in thousands of homes all across Finland. The process of making those decisions actually begins two years before the box is set to release when they meet with a group of advisors. The group is essentially a justice list. of baby stuff experts.
酸橙绿条纹和荧光黄。是的，非常有90年代的感觉。今天，安妮娜和她的同事维拉负责决定每年婴儿盒里包含什么。压力很大。她们挑选的每一件物品，无论是考拉睡袋还是恐龙牙刷，都将被芬兰成千上万个家庭在成千上万个家里使用。做出这些决定的过程实际上在盒子发布前两年就开始了，那时她们会与一组顾问会面。这组人本质上是一个……

21
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:16.000
There's a representative from the maternity and child welfare clinics, another from the Finnish Institute for Health and Welfare, one from the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health, which sounds like the same words just rearranged. And also from the Finnish safety and chemical agency,
婴儿用品专家名单。有一位来自孕产妇和儿童福利诊所的代表，一位来自芬兰健康与福利研究所的代表，一位来自社会事务与卫生部的代表（听起来像是同样的词重新排列组合），还有来自芬兰安全与化学品管理局的代表，

22
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:32.000
there's this person who we can consult about the safety issues for the items in the package. So we have these discussions about the items, which to include or what are we planning to choose. When it comes to making the final call
有这样一个人，我们可以就礼包中物品的安全问题进行咨询。所以我们会讨论这些物品，包括哪些，或者我们计划选择什么。当谈到最终决定……

23
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:54.000
about what's in each year's box, Finnish law says that all items need to, quote, provide for the health and well-being of mother and child. And most of the time, the connection between item and well-being is obvious. It would be hard to make an argument against putting in a bath thermometer, say, or a teething toy. But other times, the connection is less about need
关于每年盒子里装什么时，芬兰法律规定所有物品都需要“为母亲和孩子的健康与福祉提供保障”。大多数时候，物品与福祉之间的联系是显而易见的。很难反对放入一个洗澡温度计或一个磨牙玩具。但有时，这种联系与其说是关于需求，

24
00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:28.000
and more about encouraging certain behaviours. The box has always been about subtly influencing people's decisions. Remember, when the baby box first became a universal benefit, it was because the Finnish government wanted mothers to start going to health clinics for prenatal exams. And so despite the box's popularity, it's always carried a degree of tension between the preferences of individuals and the goals of the state. Here's Michelle Miller Fisher again. The baby box, for me, is this shining example of,
不如说是关于鼓励某些行为。婴儿盒一直以来都是为了微妙地影响人们的决定。记住，当婴儿盒首次成为全民福利时，是因为芬兰政府希望母亲们开始去健康诊所进行产前检查。因此，尽管婴儿盒很受欢迎，但它在个人偏好和国家目标之间始终存在一定程度的张力。这是米歇尔·米勒·费舍尔再次发言。对我来说，婴儿盒是……

25
00:11:28.000 --> 00:12:08.000
a welfare state done right, and yet, almost every object that is designed for the arc of human reproduction is fraught with politics. Take, for example, one item you won't find in your box, a baby bottle. That's because Finland strongly recommends breastfeeding for the first four to six months. A bottle, in that case, would be unnecessary. Another item where you can see the Finnish government trying to shape behavior is the condoms they included in the 1970s. That's when the Finnish government basically said, we think family planning is part of a healthy life for everyone. Here's a way you can make a decision about whether or not you want to get pregnant again right now.
福利国家做得好的光辉典范，然而，几乎每一件为人类生殖周期设计的物品都充满了政治色彩。以你的盒子里找不到的一件物品为例：奶瓶。这是因为芬兰强烈建议前四到六个月进行母乳喂养。在这种情况下，奶瓶是不必要的。另一件你可以看到芬兰政府试图塑造行为的物品是他们在20世纪70年代放入的避孕套。那时芬兰政府基本上说：我们认为计划生育是每个人健康生活的一部分。这是一种你可以决定是否现在想再次怀孕的方法。

26
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Not everyone appreciated the hint. Here's Tanya Vaticari, Professor of History at Tampa University. Many saw or understood that this would be a good idea. Those who were against thought that the state should not be interfering in those. kinds of ways into the family's lives. It's an issue that also came up with nappies or diapers. Early boxes included pieces of fabric that could be folded into diapers. But when the disposable kind got popular in the 1960s,
并非每个人都领情。这是坦佩雷大学历史学教授坦尼娅·瓦蒂卡里。许多人看到或理解这会是一个好主意。那些反对的人认为，国家不应该以那种方式干涉家庭生活。这个问题也出现在尿布或纸尿裤上。早期的盒子里包括可以折叠成尿布的布料。但当一次性尿布在20世纪60年代流行起来时，

27
00:12:43.000 --> 00:13:35.000
those went into the baby box instead. That is until 2006, when experts decided it would be better for the environment to switch back to cloth diapers. And people were very strongly either for or against. Many people thought that these reusable diapers being in the box was the kind of a ideological message on part of the state that people should be using these types of nappies more, which was of course true. That was the idea. So was there a sense of resentment at all or if people were feeling they were being nudged too much? Yes, there was. There was exactly. that that they were pushed towards something they did not want to adopt.
那些东西就进了婴儿盒。直到2006年，专家们认为改回布尿布对环境更好。人们对此非常强烈地支持或反对。许多人认为这些可重复使用的尿布出现在盒子里是国家的一种意识形态信息，即人们应该更多地使用这类尿布，这当然是真的。这就是初衷。那么，人们是否感到不满，或者觉得被推得太过了？是的，确实。确实有那种感觉，他们被推向了他们不想采用的东西。

28
